# HTTP behavior

- Read [OpenAPI conventions](openapi.mdc) when editing the wire contract in either service's `api/spec/`.
- Business operations obtain the client from the request context. In the prototype, ogen security resolves the caller-supplied `X-Client-ID` against PostgreSQL. In the target setup, the API Gateway authenticates the client and supplies this header. Missing or unknown IDs yield `401`. A database failure during lookup yields `500`.
- Preserve `X-Request-ID` where specified. Map unexpected failures to a generic problem response. Keep SQL errors and other implementation details out of the response.
- `RunAPI` wraps admission and the router with `RequestTimeout` so queue wait shares the request budget. Full queue or slot wait expiry returns `429`. Request deadline expiry returns `503`.
- Serve `/health/live` and `/health/ready` only on the internal loopback listeners. Keep the probe ports unpublished in Compose.
- Notifier API readiness executes `SELECT 1` under the configured database operation timeout. Its probe requests keep the HTTP request deadline and run outside admission. Emulator probes require only its HTTP server and initialized in-memory store.
- Worker readiness executes `SELECT 1` through the worker's database pool under the configured database operation timeout. Worker liveness is independent of PostgreSQL.
- Compose checks `/health/ready` from inside each container. Keep the route, loopback port and probe timeout aligned when changing startup.
